-- T6 regression, INTRA-directive: temporal-context leakage through thunk
-- memoization within a SINGLE #EVAL.
--
-- The cross-directive temporal-thunk-leak-basic/-iterators fixtures are
-- MASKED by the per-directive heap rebuild ('forEachDirectiveFreshHeap'
-- re-thunks top-level CAFs between directives), so they pass with or without
-- the WHNFWhen fingerprint machinery. Here BOTH the override force and the
-- ambient read of ONE shared binding happen inside ONE directive, over one
-- heap: only the fingerprint-guarded re-force keeps them apart. If the T6
-- machinery regresses to unconditional memoization, every MINUS below
-- collapses to 0 while the cross-directive goldens stay green.
--
-- The test harness pins the clock: JL4_FIXED_NOW = 2025-01-31T15:45:30Z,
-- so ambient DATE_SERIAL TODAY is the serial of 2025-01-31.

TIMEZONE IS "Etc/UTC"

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Case 1: forced under the override first, read ambient second
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`serial snapshot 1` MEANS DATE_SERIAL TODAY

-- expected: serial(2020-01-01) MINUS serial(2025-01-31) = -1857
-- (a T6 regression leaks the override value into the ambient read: 0)
#EVAL (`EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020)) `serial snapshot 1`) MINUS `serial snapshot 1`

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Case 2: forced ambiently first, read under the override second
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`serial snapshot 2` MEANS DATE_SERIAL TODAY

-- expected: serial(2025-01-31) MINUS serial(2020-01-01) = 1857
-- (a T6 regression leaks the ambient value into the override read: 0)
#EVAL `serial snapshot 2` MINUS (`EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020)) `serial snapshot 2`)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Case 3: nested overrides within one directive
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`serial snapshot 3` MEANS DATE_SERIAL TODAY

-- expected: serial(2020-01-01) MINUS serial(2010-01-01) = 3652
-- (a T6 regression collapses this to 0 too)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020)) (`serial snapshot 3` MINUS (`EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2010)) `serial snapshot 3`))

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Case 4 (sharing sanity): two reads under ONE context still share
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`serial snapshot 4` MEANS DATE_SERIAL TODAY

-- expected: 0 — matching contexts serve the cache; no spurious re-forcing
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020)) (`serial snapshot 4` MINUS `serial snapshot 4`)
